{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To find the smallest bouncy prime, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the function \\( f(n) \\), which is the sum of the positive divisors of \\( n \\).\n2. Determine the value of \\( f(n) \\) for a prime number \\( n \\).\n3. Check if \\( f(f(n)) \\) is a prime number.\n4. Identify the smallest prime \\( n \\) for which \\( f(f(n)) \\) is also prime.\n\nFirst, let's consider the smallest prime numbers and calculate \\( f(n) \\) and \\( f(f(n)) \\):\n\n1. For \\( n = 2 \\):\n   - The divisors of 2 are 1 and 2, so \\( f(2) = 1 + 2 = 3 \\).\n   - The divisors of 3 are 1 and 3, so \\( f(3) = 1 + 3 = 4 \\).\n   - Since 4 is not a prime number, 2 is not a bouncy prime.\n\n2. For \\( n = 3 \\):\n   - The divisors of 3 are 1 and 3, so \\( f(3) = 1 + 3 = 4 \\).\n   - Since 4 is not a prime number, 3 is not a bouncy prime.\n\n3. For \\( n = 5 \\):\n   - The divisors of 5 are 1 and 5, so \\( f(5) = 1 + 5 = 6 \\).\n   - The divisors of 6 are 1, 2, 3, and 6, so \\( f(6) = 1 + 2 + 3 + 6 = 12 \\).\n   - Since 12 is not a prime number, 5 is not a bouncy prime.\n\n4. For \\( n = 7 \\):\n   - The divisors of 7 are 1 and 7, so \\( f(7) = 1 + 7 = 8 \\).\n   - The divisors of 8 are 1, 2, 4, and 8, so \\( f(8) = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 \\).\n   - Since 15 is not a prime number, 7 is not a bouncy prime.\n\n5. For \\( n = 11 \\):\n   - The divisors of 11 are 1 and 11, so \\( f(11) = 1 + 11 = 12 \\).\n   - The divisors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, and 12, so \\( f(12) = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 6 + 12 = 28 \\).\n   - Since 28 is not a prime number, 11 is not a bouncy prime.\n\n6. For \\( n = 13 \\):\n   - The divisors of 13 are 1 and 13, so \\( f(13) = 1 + 13 = 14 \\).\n   - The divisors of 14 are 1, 2, 7, and 14, so \\( f(14) = 1 + 2 + 7 + 14 = 24 \\).\n   - Since 24 is not a prime number, 13 is not a bouncy prime.\n\n7. For \\( n = 17 \\):\n   - The divisors of 17 are 1 and 17, so \\( f(17) = 1 + 17 = 18 \\).\n   - The divisors of 18 are 1, 2, 3,